    <item>
      <title>Wine 11.23 Released With Support For ARM64 macOS, AVX In 32-bit Emulation On ARM64</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.23-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.23-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 28 Nov 2026 01:31:43 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="WINE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/wine.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; We are getting close to the Wine 12.0 release candidate phase but out today is Wine 11.23 as the latest bi-weekly development release continuing to land new features into Wine. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.23 adds initial support for ARM64 on macOS. Finally. Various Wine build changes were needed for ARM64 macOS as well as some NTDLL changes for properly handling ARM64 macOS with the Apple Silicon Macs of the past six years. The ARM64 support depends on macOS 27. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Also notable on the ARM64 side with Wine 11.23 is adding Advanced Vector Extensions (AVX) support in 32-bit emulation on 64-bit ARM. Wine 11.22 introduced &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.22-Released"&gt;AVX emulation on ARM64&lt;/a&gt; for 64-bit (x86_64) while now it's working for 32-bit software too. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine 11.23 also pulls in the new &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/VKD3D-2.2-Released"&gt;VKD3D 2.2&lt;/a&gt; release with its many improvements for Direct3D 12 over the Vulkan API. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Wine 11.23 release also provides better support for generating C++ headers in WIDL. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Last but not least is the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-OpenGL-Cross-Proc-Render"&gt;remote OpenGL swapchain image presentation support&lt;/a&gt; in Wine 11.23 that also now allows the Chromium Embedded Framework (CEF) to work nicely on Wayland under Wine. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There is also 40 known bug fixes in Wine 11.23 that help a variety of Windows games and applications. The complete list of fixes and patches with Wine 11.23 can be found via the release announcement on &lt;a href="https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/releases/wine-11.23"&gt;WineHQ.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>GMKtec EVO-X2 AMD Ryzen AI Max+ Strix Halo Mini PC</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/review/gmktec-evo-x2</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/review/gmktec-evo-x2</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 21:33:54 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_1_med" alt="GMKtec box" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For those in the market for an AMD Ryzen AI Max+ mini PC but not able to procure the likes of the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Ryzen+AI+Halo"&gt;Ryzen AI Halo&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Framework+Desktop"&gt;Framework Desktop&lt;/a&gt; in your region or with elevated pricing, another Strix Halo option that's been available is the GMKtec EVO-X2. I recently received a review sample of the GMKtec EVO-X2 and put it through a variety of benchmarks in seeing how the performance stacks up against the flagship Ryzen AI Halo and Framework Desktop offerings as well as the BOSGAME VTA-439 powered by the AMD Ryzen AI 9 HX 470 for reference plus also the Dell Pro Max GB10 powered by the NVIDIA GB10 SoC.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_2_med" alt="GMKtec EVO-X2 packaging" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The GMKtec EVO-X2 is a Strix Halo mini PC using the Ryzen AI Max+ 395 and options for either 64GB or 128GB of LPDDR5X RAM plus 1TB or 2TB of NVMe SSD storage. The EVO-X2 has dual M2.2 2280 slots for supporting up to two NVMe SSDs if you need a lot of storage or looking to upgrade on your own after the fact. Accessing the mini PC can be done via removing the rubber feet at the bottom for the hidden screws.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_3_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_3_med" alt="GMKtec EVO-X2 mini PC" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Front panel I/O ports on this AMD Ryzen AI Max+ mini PC include two USB-A 3.2 Gen 2, one 3.5 mm combo audio jack, one USB 4.0 Type-C connector, and an SD card reader.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_4_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_4_med" alt="GMKtec EVO-X2 front" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;At the rear of the GMKtec EVO-X2 is a DisplayPort 1.4 output, one HDMI 2.1 output, one USB-A 3.2 Gen 2, two USB-A 2.0, one USB 4.0 Type-C, one audio jack, DC power input, and 2.5G Ethernet RJ45 connector. The mini PC has WiFi via a Mediatek MT7925 chipset as well as Bluetooth 5.4.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_5_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_5_med" alt="GMKtec EVO-X2 rear" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Microsoft Windows 11 Professional shipped on this particular review unit while GMKtec does list "Ubuntu" as being supported too. I already ran some comparison benchmarks for those interested within &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/windows-ubuntu2610-strix-halo"&gt;Windows 11 vs. Ubuntu 26.10 Linux Performance On AMD Ryzen AI Max+&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_6_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_6_med" alt="GMKtec EVO-X2 bottom" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;As the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Strix+Halo"&gt;Strix Halo&lt;/a&gt; Linux support has been in good shape since launch sans the likes of web camera issues with select models, there isn't too much worry about for Linux from the Strix Halo mini PC perspective. If you are running any modern Linux distribution within the past year you should be in good shape with the GMKtec EVO-X2. I've tested this Strix Halo PC with Ubuntu 26.04, Ubuntu 26.10, Fedora Workstation 45, CachyOS, and others without any troubles.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_7_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_7_med" alt="GMKtec EVO-X2 with power supply" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For evaluating the performance out of the GMKtec EVO-X2, I compared it to other Strix Halo hardware I have available and some other similar mini PCs:&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;- Dell Pro Max GB10&lt;br /&gt;  - Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395&lt;br /&gt;  - Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395&lt;br /&gt;  - BOSGAME VTA-439 Ryzen AI 9 HX 470&lt;br /&gt;  - GMKtec EVO-X2S Ryzen AI Max+ 395&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_8_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=gmktec-evo-x2&amp;image=gmktec_x2_8_med" alt="GMKtec EVO-X2 top ventilation" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The Dell Pro Max GB10 was tested with the official Ubuntu-derived NVIDIA OS, the Ryzen AI Halo was running the official Ryzen AI Developer Platform OS, and the others were tested on clean Ubuntu 26.10 installs. For all configurations they were tested with their "performance" platform profile.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="https://phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/result.svgz" alt="AMD Ryzen AI Max Strix Halo Mini PCs" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;In addition to looking at the raw performance, various system thermals and power sensors were also logged during the testing process.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-cpu-blas-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;In first looking at the Llama.cpp performance from just the CPU perspective, the Strix Halo mini PCs were faster than the Dell Pro Max GB10 and Ryzen AI 9 HX 470 understandably. The GMKtec EVO-X2S was slower though than the Framework Desktop and Ryzen AI Halo PCs with the same SoC.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-cpu-blas-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-tg1-2.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;When looking at the CPU power consumption, we see that the Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was pulling less than the other two Strix Halo PCs. Even though all of these mini PCs were running in the "performance" platform profile.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-cpu-blas-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-tg1-8.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;This did lead to a similar performance-per-Watt among those Strix Halo mini PCs. The Dell Pro Max GB10 didn't have any CPU/SoC power metrics exposed.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-cpu-blas-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-pp2.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Prompt Processing 2048. GMKtec EVO-X2S Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Interestingly for the prompt processing performance with GPT-OSS 20B (the size being limited to also accommodate the BOSGAME VTA-439), the GMKtec mini PC came out ahead of the other Strix Halo PCs.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-cpu-blas-deepseek-r1-distill-llama-8b-q8_0-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-cpu-blas-deepseek-r1-distill-llama-8b-q8_0-pp2.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B-Q8_0, Test: Prompt Processing 2048. GMKtec EVO-X2S Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-cpu-blas-minimax-m25-ud-tq1_0-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: MiniMax-M2.5-UD-TQ1_0, Test: Text Generation 128. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-cpu-blas-minimax-m25-ud-tq1_0-pp2.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: CPU BLAS, Model: MiniMax-M2.5-UD-TQ1_0, Test: Prompt Processing 2048. GMKtec EVO-X2S Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For DeepSeek R1 and other models, there was a similar scenario of the GMKtec EVO-X2S falling behind for text generation performance but coming out in front for prompt processing.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-amd-rocm-hip-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-tg1.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm HIP, Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Text Generation 128. GMKtec EVO-X2S Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/llamacpp-amd-rocm-hip-gpt-oss-20b-q8_0-pp2.svgz" alt="Llama.cpp benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm HIP, Model: gpt-oss-20b-Q8_0, Test: Prompt Processing 2048. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;When leveraging the ROCm HIP back-end with Llama.cpp, the results between these Strix Halo mini PCs were quite close.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/pytorch-cpu-32-resnet-50.svgz" alt="PyTorch benchmark with settings of Device: CPU, Batch Size: 32, Model: ResNet-50. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For PyTorch on the CPUs, the GMKtec EVO-X2S was slightly behind the Ryzen AI Halo and Framework Desktop. Even with all running in the performance mode, the GMKtec was tending to pull less power than the other two Strix Halo SoC.s&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/mixbench-amd-rocm-hip-spg.svgz" alt="Mixbench benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm HIP, Single Precision GFLOPS. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/mixbench-amd-rocm-hip-pspg.svgz" alt="Mixbench benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm HIP, Packed Single Precision GFLOPS. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/mixbench-amd-rocm-hip-dpg.svgz" alt="Mixbench benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm HIP, Double Precision GFLOPS. GMKtec EVO-X2S Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/mixbench-amd-rocm-hip-hpg.svgz" alt="Mixbench benchmark with settings of Backend: AMD ROCm HIP, Half Precision GFLOPS. GMKtec EVO-X2S Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For Mixbench with its ROCm HIP back-end tended to be quite similar performance across all three Strix Halo mini PCs.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/hashcat-sha1.svgz" alt="Hashcat benchmark with settings of Benchmark: SHA1. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/hashcat-sha-256.svgz" alt="Hashcat benchmark with settings of Benchmark: SHA-256. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/hashcat-sha-512.svgz" alt="Hashcat benchmark with settings of Benchmark: SHA-512. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/hashcat-7-zip.svgz" alt="Hashcat benchmark with settings of Benchmark: 7-Zip. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/hashcat-k5e2tr.svgz" alt="Hashcat benchmark with settings of Benchmark: Kerberos 5 + etype 23 + TGS-REP. GMKtec EVO-X2S Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For Hashcat with OpenCL, the Ryzen AI Halo and Framework Desktop were continuing to deliver more performance than the GMKtec mini PC but at higher power levels.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/gravitymark-3840-x-2160-vulkan.svgz" alt="GravityMark benchmark with settings of Resolution: 3840 x 2160, Renderer: Vulkan. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/gravitymark-3840-x-2160-vulkan-ray-tracing.svgz" alt="GravityMark benchmark with settings of Resolution: 3840 x 2160, Renderer: Vulkan Ray-Tracing. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For the GravityMark Vulkan graphics benchmark, the GMKtec was right behind the Framework Desktop for a solid second place finish. The slower Ryzen AI Halo performance is likely due to its Ryzen AI Developer Platform OS having an older Mesa driver stack.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/electrobench-3840-x-2160-ouvc.svgz" alt="ElectroBench benchmark with settings of Resolution: 3840 x 2160, Scene: Ocean Under Volumetric Clouds. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/electrobench-3840-x-2160-warcspr.svgz" alt="ElectroBench benchmark with settings of Resolution: 3840 x 2160, Scene: White-And-Red Checkerboard-Sky Pool Room. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/electrobench-3840-x-2160-ucaad.svgz" alt="ElectroBench benchmark with settings of Resolution: 3840 x 2160, Scene: Utility Corridor At Amber Dusk. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The Framework Desktop was also leading in the ElectroBench OpenGL benchmark test cases.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/timed-linux-kernel-compilation-allmodconfig.svgz" alt="Timed Linux Kernel Compilation benchmark with settings of Build: allmodconfig. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/timed-linux-kernel-compilation-allmodconfig-2.svgz" alt="Timed Linux Kernel Compilation benchmark with settings of Build: allmodconfig. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/timed-linux-kernel-compilation-allmodconfig-8.svgz" alt="Timed Linux Kernel Compilation benchmark with settings of Build: allmodconfig. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/timed-godot-game-engine-compilation-time-to-compile.svgz" alt="Timed Godot Game Engine Compilation benchmark with settings of Time To Compile. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;    &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/timed-godot-game-engine-compilation-time-to-compile-2.svgz" alt="Timed Godot Game Engine Compilation benchmark with settings of Time To Compile. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/timed-godot-game-engine-compilation-time-to-compile-8.svgz" alt="Timed Godot Game Engine Compilation benchmark with settings of Time To Compile. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For those considering any of these mini PCs for code compilation boxes. the GMKtec EVO-X2S was again slightly slower than the Framework Desktop and Ryzen AI Halo even in the performance profile. The CPU power consumption showed the GMKtec averaging around 82 Watts and a peak of 120 Watts compared to the other Strix Halo PCs averaging at 120 Watts with a peak around 160 Watts. In any event this led the GMKtec EVO-X2 to having a competitive performance-per-Watt.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/timed-nodejs-compilation-time-to-compile.svgz" alt="Timed Node.js Compilation benchmark with settings of Time To Compile. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/timed-nodejs-compilation-time-to-compile-2.svgz" alt="Timed Node.js Compilation benchmark with settings of Time To Compile. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/timed-nodejs-compilation-time-to-compile-8.svgz" alt="Timed Node.js Compilation benchmark with settings of Time To Compile. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/pogocache-1100.svgz" alt="Pogocache benchmark with settings of Set To Get Ratio: 1:100. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/pogocache-1100-2.svgz" alt="Pogocache benchmark with settings of Set To Get Ratio: 1:100. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/pogocache-1100-8.svgz" alt="Pogocache benchmark with settings of Set To Get Ratio: 1:100. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/pogocache-15.svgz" alt="Pogocache benchmark with settings of Set To Get Ratio: 1:5. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/pogocache-15-2.svgz" alt="Pogocache benchmark with settings of Set To Get Ratio: 1:5. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/pogocache-15-8.svgz" alt="Pogocache benchmark with settings of Set To Get Ratio: 1:5. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/openssl-sha256.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: SHA256. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/openssl-sha256-2.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: SHA256. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/openssl-sha256-8.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: SHA256. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/openssl-aes-128-gcm.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: AES-128-GCM. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/openssl-chacha20.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: ChaCha20. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/openssl-chacha20-poly1305.svgz" alt="OpenSSL benchmark with settings of Algorithm: ChaCha20-Poly1305. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The Strix Halo mini PCs can be quite capable for SOHO server type scenarios.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/libxsmm-128.svgz" alt="libxsmm benchmark with settings of M N K: 128. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/libxsmm-128-2.svgz" alt="libxsmm benchmark with settings of M N K: 128. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/libxsmm-128-8.svgz" alt="libxsmm benchmark with settings of M N K: 128. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/namd-sw106a.svgz" alt="NAMD benchmark with settings of Input: STMV with 1,066,628 Atoms. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/namd-sw106a-2.svgz" alt="NAMD benchmark with settings of Input: STMV with 1,066,628 Atoms. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/namd-sw106a-8.svgz" alt="NAMD benchmark with settings of Input: STMV with 1,066,628 Atoms. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/svt-av1-preset-13-bosphorus-4k.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 13, Input: Bosphorus 4K. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/svt-av1-preset-8-bosphorus-4k.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 8, Input: Bosphorus 4K. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/svt-av1-preset-3-bosphorus-4k.svgz" alt="SVT-AV1 benchmark with settings of Encoder Mode: Preset 3, Input: Bosphorus 4K. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/openapv-fastest-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="OpenAPV benchmark with settings of Encoder Preset: Fastest, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/openapv-medium-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="OpenAPV benchmark with settings of Encoder Preset: Medium, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/openapv-slow-beauty-4k-10-bit.svgz" alt="OpenAPV benchmark with settings of Encoder Preset: Slow, Input: Beauty 4K 10-bit. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/stockfish-chess-benchmark.svgz" alt="Stockfish benchmark with settings of Chess Benchmark. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/stockfish-chess-benchmark-2.svgz" alt="Stockfish benchmark with settings of Chess Benchmark. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/stockfish-chess-benchmark-8.svgz" alt="Stockfish benchmark with settings of Chess Benchmark. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/coremark-coremark-size-666-ips.svgz" alt="Coremark benchmark with settings of CoreMark Size 666, Iterations Per Second. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/coremark-coremark-size-666-ips-2.svgz" alt="Coremark benchmark with settings of CoreMark Size 666, Iterations Per Second. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/coremark-coremark-size-666-ips-8.svgz" alt="Coremark benchmark with settings of CoreMark Size 666, Iterations Per Second. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The GMKtec EVO-X2S in the performance profile was still trailing the other Strix Halo mini PCs in raw performance but with the lower CPU power consumption led to competitive performance-per-Watt.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/sysbench-cpu.svgz" alt="Sysbench benchmark with settings of Test: CPU. Ryzen AI Halo Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/sysbench-ram---memory.svgz" alt="Sysbench benchmark with settings of Test: RAM / Memory. Dell Pro Max GB10 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Here's a look at various system vitals over the course of the dozens of benchmarks conducted:&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/cpu-power-consumption-monitoring-overview-acpcm.svgz" alt="CPU Power Consumption Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated CPU Power Consumption Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With all of the mini PCs tested in their performance profile, the GMKtec EVO-X2 was consuming 80 Watts on average with a peak of 132 Watts. Meanwhile the Framework Desktop and Ryzen AI Halo were quite similar with a 105 Watt average and a peak around 180 Watts. If you are more concerned about power efficiency than the raw throughput, the GMKtec EVO-X2 was delivering very competitive performance-per-Watt.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/cpu-peak-freq-monitoring-overview-acpfm.svgz" alt="CPU Peak Freq Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated CPU Peak Freq Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The GMKtec EVO-X2 was still topping out at up to 5.1GHz but with the reduced power limits was typically peaking lower than the Framework Desktop and Ryzen AI Halo mini PCs.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/cpu-temp-monitoring-overview-actm.svgz" alt="CPU Temp Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated CPU Temp Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The lower CPU power consumption of the GMKtec EVO-X2 did lead to it having the lowest core operating temperatures.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/gpu-temp-monitoring-overview-agtm.svgz" alt="GPU Temp Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated GPU Temp Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;And the GPU temperatures across all of the tested mini PCs.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/drive-temp-monitoring-overview-adtm.svgz" alt="Drive Temp Monitoring Overview benchmark with settings of Accumulated Drive Temp Monitoring." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The GMKtec EVO-X2 was keeping all of the components cool enough with no thermal concerns to note.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The geo mean based on the tests that ran successfully across all of the tested mini PCs:&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/amd-ryzen-ai-max-strix-halo-mini-pcs/geometric-mean-of-all-test-results-result-composite-aramshmp.svgz" alt="Geometric Mean Of All Test Results benchmark with settings of Result Composite, AMD Ryzen AI Max Strix Halo Mini PCs. Framework Desktop Ryzen AI Max+ 395 was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Across the mix of workloads the GMKtec EVO-X2 was coming in right behind the Ryzen AI Halo and Framework Desktop for raw performance but typically matching or exceeding in performance-per-Watt. Beyond the performance, all of these Strix Halo mini PCs were running great with Linux with no compatibility issues to note. (Update: the performance delta between the GMKtec EVO-X2 and other Strix Halo PCs were more narrowed when hitting the physical "fan mode" button on the side of the EVO-X2. It would be nice if that fan mode was just tied into ACPI platform profile behavior or similar for those that may want to run their mini PC racked up in a home lab or similar to avoid the need to physically press the button as desired if it were just tied into the platform profile behavior like with the other Strix Halo PCs not possessing a physical fan mode switch button.)&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The GMKtec EVO-X2 as of writing starts out at around $2199 USD for the configuration with 64GB of RAM and 1TB of storage. Those wishing to learn more about this mini PC can do so at &lt;a href="https://www.gmktec.com/products/amd-ryzen%E2%84%A2-ai-max-395-evo-x2-ai-mini-pc"&gt;GMKtec.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>New "Earthqaic" Gallium3D Driver Merged For Mesa 27.0</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Earthqaic-Gallium3D-Mesa-27.0</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Earthqaic-Gallium3D-Mesa-27.0</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 20:21:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="MESA" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/mesa.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Merged today to Mesa Git is a brand new Gallium3D driver: Earthqaic. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Earthqaic Gallium3D driver is developed by Qualcomm for their Cloud AI accelerator devices, the "QAIC" portion. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In targeting the Qualcomm Cloud AI accelerator hardware, this Gallium3D driver isn't for conventional graphics use cases but rather exclusively targeting Gallium3D's Teflon framework and Rusticl. &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Teflon"&gt;Teflon&lt;/a&gt; provides for some AI acceleration while &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Rusticl"&gt;Rusticl&lt;/a&gt; is the Rust-based OpenCL driver implementation. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=earthqaic" alt="Earhqaic" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Before getting too excited about Earthqaic if you happen to have Qualcomm Cloud AI hardware, this Gallium3D code right now is very experimental and primitive: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Support for teflon and rusticl is limited. The driver currently implements tensor addition and convolution and can compile and execute a simple "hello world" OpenCL kernel...Earthqaic is an experimental proof of concept and stability issues may arise."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In any event it's great to see Qualcomm working on this QAIC driver for Mesa. This complements &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Qualcomm-QAIC-For-Linux-6.4"&gt;the QAIC kernel driver in the Linux mainline tree&lt;/a&gt; going back a few years. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This Earthqaic driver will be part of the Mesa 27.0 release coming out in Q1.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux Dropping Last Of The 1980's VersaModular Eurocard "VME" vme_user Code Over AI/LLM Noise</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Drops-VME-Bus-User</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Drops-VME-Bus-User</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 15:50:44 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="HARDWARE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/hardware.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The Linux kernel is removing more old driver code due to AI/LLMs generating more noise over this unused, obsolete code within the source tree. Following &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-To-Drop-ARCnet-ISA-PCMCIA"&gt;various old ISA and PCMCIA code removed&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.1-Removes-Old-Net"&gt;ham radio, ISDN, and other old network code&lt;/a&gt;, the latest vintage artifact set to be stripped out of the Linux kernel is around the VersaModular Eurocard "VME" bus vme_user driver from the 1980s.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The Linux kernel ahead of tomorrow's Linux 7.4-rc5 release has seen a post-merge-window pull request for removing the last of the VersaModular Eurocard "VME" parallel computer bus code's vme_user driver for user-space access. The "vme_user" driver that for years has been within the Linux kernel's staging area -- &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-VMEbus-Back-To-Staging"&gt;the VME code was demoted to staging in 2022&lt;/a&gt;.  There is no other kernel code left in the tree that is dependent upon the vme_user driver and with it not being maintained and for obsolete hardware, it's time to drop it given that it's been causing AI/LLM noise on the mailing lists.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=vme_kconfig" alt="VME Kconfigs" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/tencent_6DFC9819B65A274390D9E2A01B3C1C9D160A@qq.com/"&gt;The patch&lt;/a&gt; removing it drops nearly 9k lines of code. The rationale on the removal patch:  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"The staging VME userspace drivers are orphaned and Greg KH requested a patch to remove them instead of continuing to fix staging-only issues.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Remove the driver, its documentation, Kconfig and Makefile entries, the VME MAINTAINERS section, and stale VME options from the powerpc 86xx config."&lt;/blockquote&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;In today's &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/awmLIGoX6sME2IJQ@kroah.com/"&gt;staging pull&lt;/a&gt;, Greg Kroah-Hartman confirmed the AI/LLM noise:  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"Here is a single staging driver patch that removes the vme_user driver. There is no more users of it and the LLMs are falling all over themselves attempting to find all of the "security bugs" in this driver, despite no one ever actually use it.  So just remove it to keep from wasting everyone's time.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;This removal has been in linux-next for quite a while with no reported issues."&lt;/blockquote&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;So barring any last minute issues, the vme_user code will be removed ahead of tomorrow's Linux 7.4-rc5 kernel release.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux Mint 27 Beta Released With New Apps, Full Wayland Support</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Mint-27-Beta</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Mint-27-Beta</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 15:37:29 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="OPERATING SYSTEMS" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/operatingsystems.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Ahead of the official release expected around Christmas, the beta of Linux Mint 27 was announced today for testing. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Linux Mint 27 Beta is now available for this popular desktop Linux distribution derived from Ubuntu. With Linux Mint 27 they are transitioning to the Ubuntu 26.04 LTS base. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Linux Miny 27 Beta brings the latest Cinnamon desktop environment and includes a number of new Linux Mint original apps like the Clockenstein calendar app, Velocity as their new terminal app, Superlight as their new system monitor, Unfold as a lightweight archive manager, Xepub as a new EPUB book reader,  new system administration capabilities, restored hibernation support, and much more. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=linux_mint_27_beta" alt="Linux Mint 27 beta" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Linux Mint 27 Beta also features full Wayland support with the Cinnamon 6.9 desktop, 3D acceleration, HiDPI, fractional scaling, keyboard layouts, and other typical features are now all working on Wayland. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on today's Linux Mint 27 Beta release via &lt;a href="https://blog.linuxmint.com/?p=5120"&gt;LinuxMint.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>systemd 263-rc1 Ships Initial Rust Code, Experimental Pseudo TTY Broker &amp; Manifest</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/systemd-263-rc1</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/systemd-263-rc1</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 11:42:12 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="SYSTEMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/systemd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Systemd 263-rc1 was just tagged in gearing up for this next major release. Systemd 263 brings initial support for using the Rust programming language for creating new Rust programs. Plus systemd 263 ships with a new pseudo TTY broker service, a manifest tool, a new keyring service and much more. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Some of the highlights of systemd 263 include: &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;- As previously covered, &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/systemd-Merges-Rust-Programs"&gt;systemd now has support for Rust programs&lt;/a&gt;. Rust code is now available in the systemd leaf code but not in the systemd core library code yet. The intent is also on Rust being used for new development and not rewriting existing C code in Rust just for the sake of it. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- A new experimental pseudo TTY broker.  The systemd-ptybrokerd service allows pseudo TTYs and keeps them open independently of the client that requested it. This, for example, can be used to disconnect from a container console and reconnect later on without stopping the container. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- A new systemd-manifest tool to generate and verify file manifests for directory trees. The default format is the UAPI.16 File Manifest. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- A new systemd-keyring-setup.service to enroll X.509 certificates into the kernel's dm-verity, fs-verity, and BPF keyrings early during boot followed by sealing the keyrings. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- Reading and verifying of large systemd journals is now faster -- can be about 1.7x faster for the default journalctl output. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- The systemd-networkd and networkctl have been merged into a single multi-call binary for a smaller installation footprint. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- systemd-resolved can now verify ML-DSA-44 DNSSEC signatures. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- A new &lt;em&gt;SecureBits=&lt;/em&gt; setting for system.conf and user.conf to set secure bits on the service manager itself. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- A new unit setting of &lt;em&gt;IOuringFilter=&lt;/em&gt; to set an allow list or deny list of IO_uring operations. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;- systemd-report has new metric providers for reporting hardware temperatures, free space on writable file systems, SMART information, and NVMe disk health details. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the plethora of systemd changes and new additions coming to systemd 263 can be found via today's &lt;a href="https://github.com/systemd/systemd/releases/tag/v263-rc1"&gt;263-rc1 announcement&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Intel's Vulkan Linux Driver Now Enables Efficient 64-bit Mode "e64" By Default</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Efficient-64-bit-e64-ANV</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Efficient-64-bit-e64-ANV</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 11:26:10 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="INTEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/intel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Debuting with the upcoming Intel Nova Lake P processors with their Xe3P integrated graphics is &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Iris-Efficient-64-bit"&gt;a new efficient 64-bit addressing mode&lt;/a&gt; "e64". This efficient 64-bit addressing mode for the future Intel GPUs provides native 64-bit shader memory addressing as a replacement to the 32-bit offset mode used by Intel GPU shaders up to now. As of the latest Mesa Git code, the Intel ANV Vulkan driver is enabling the e64 mode by default. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In preparing for the upcoming Nova Lake P processors, Mesa 27.0-devel now enabled the efficient 64-bit mode by default for those Xe3P graphics. Nova Lake P supports both the efficient 64-bit mode and the now-legacy 32-bit mode where as Intel graphics hardware beyond NVL-P will presumably drop the 32-bit offset mode entirely. For Nova Lake P though now that the e64 driver code is in order, Intel is able to enable it by default. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=intel_e64_default" alt="Intel e64 mode enabled by default patch" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/46751"&gt;This merge request&lt;/a&gt; hitting Mesa Git yesterday brought bug fixes for the e64 code path in ANV and ultimately is now good enough for enabling it by default. The pass rate is said to be better now with e64 on than off. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;It will be exciting to check out the Intel Xe3P graphics in the new year on Linux. It's looking like both the Linux kernel and Mesa driver support should be in order ahead of the actual product launches.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>ZenSMU Provides A Rust CLI/TUI &amp; GUI For AMD Ryzen Monitoring/Tweaking</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/ZenSMU-Linux-TUI-GUI</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/ZenSMU-Linux-TUI-GUI</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 11:05:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; In the absence of any official AMD Ryzen Master utility for Linux to extensively monitor your Ryzen processor and overclocking/tweaking the CPU, there are a few unofficial, open-source utilities out there that aim for a similar intent. One of the new ones is ZenSMU that provides both a CLI/TUI interface and a proper Linux desktop GUI. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;ZenSMU is a Rust-based live TUI and desktop GUI for controlling and monitoring the AMD Ryzen SMU . There is power and thermal limits options, curve optimizer, telemetry exporting support, and other adjustments possible. This has been tested from Zen 1 to Zen 5 processors. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=zensmu_1" alt="ZenSMU TUI" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This work depends upon the out-of-tree and separate &lt;a href="https://gitlab.com/leogx9r/ryzen_smu"&gt;ryzen_smu&lt;/a&gt; kernel driver. That kernel driver is what exposes all of the AMD Ryzen system management unity capabilities. It's been three years since that out of tree driver has been updated but still appears to be working even for the newer Ryzen CPUs. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=zensmu_2" alt="ZenSMU GUI" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;It's unfortunate there isn't any similar driver in the mainline Linux kernel yet. That could be changing soon though as I wrote about last month in an official AMD Linux effort: &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMD-AOD-Overclocking-Linux"&gt;AMD Working On Ryzen Overclocking Driver For Linux&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In any event those wanting to check out ZenSMU can find all the MIT licensed code on &lt;a href="https://github.com/brauliobo/zensmu"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Intel Compute Runtime 26.44.40734.8 Had OpenCL 3.1 But Reverted, More Performance</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Compute-26.44.40734.8</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Compute-26.44.40734.8</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 10:52:29 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="INTEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/intel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Intel on Thursday released their Compute Runtime 26.44.40734.8 release as the latest feature update to this open-source GPU compute stack used on Linux and Windows. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Intel Compute Runtime 26.44.40734.8 delivers various performance optimizations for the Intel GPU D Series "Crescent Island" like splitting upstream writes to help with better performance. There has also been some generic code refactoring to optimize the compilation of Level Zero. There is also now a staging copy used for images in Level Zero, copying the host pointer on the CPU when the buffer is backed by host memory, and other performance tweaks. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There was also a &lt;a href="https://github.com/intel/compute-runtime/commit/4d45295eeeb62ed63fd96d325a5f7f5cd1bc0c3b"&gt;patch&lt;/a&gt; to enable OpenCL 3.1 support across all supported Intel graphics generations. This upgrade from OpenCL 3.0 to OpenCL 3.1 ended up being reveerted. The &lt;a href="https://github.com/intel/compute-runtime/commit/22a5057cba3574d0523dd7754ddbf53392032e69"&gt;revert patch&lt;/a&gt; didn't elaborate why they went back to OpenCL 3.0 being exposed. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=intel_opencl_31" alt="Intel OpenCL 3.1" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The OpenCL LEO code that builds OpenCL atop the Level Zero APIs also continues to be enhanced ahead of the used-by-default status with upcoming Nova Lake processors. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;An additional Panther Lake device ID was also added to the Compute Runtime:  0xB0A1. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There were also various bug fixes and other enhancements throughout this Intel GPU compute stack. Downloads and the full list of 400+ patches in this release via &lt;a href="https://github.com/intel/compute-runtime/releases/tag/26.44.40734.8"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Reminder: Black Friday Deal &amp; The Unfortunate Ad Network Situation This Year</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Black-Friday-Week-2026</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Black-Friday-Week-2026</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 10:44:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="PHORONIX" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/phoronix.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; To all Phoronix readers a happy holidays. Just a friendly reminder about the ongoing &amp;quot;Black Friday&amp;quot; special at Phoronix to help support the site after a particularly struggling year while continuing to provide daily Linux hardware reviews and open-source news.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;If you have enjoyed the more than 3,125+ news articles so far this year and 200+ Linux hardware reviews this calendar year with a lot of interesting content, running this week is the annual Phoronix Premium end-of-year / "Black Friday" promotion to help support the site while enjoying ad-free access, multi-page articles on a single page, and other benefits while helping Phoronix continue as it approaches its 23rd birthday next June. Unfortunately, this year has been the toughest yet.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Running through next week on end of day 1 December (any timezone, not particularly strict) is the annual Phoronix.com holiday / Black Friday / Cyber Week promotion. You can join &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/phoronix-premium"&gt;Phoronix Premium&lt;/a&gt; at a discounted rate to help support the continued Linux hardware reviews/testing, open-source news, and more, and join the sub-1% club of those readers that actually subscribe to Phoronix Premium. Web based advertisements have traditionally been the main source of advertisements for running Phoronix.com since 2004 while in more recent years has been under increasing pressure due to companies devoting more of their ad spends to social networks, increased ad-blocking, etc.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;This year has been the worst on record for Phoronix sustainability due to Amazon Ads and co (those just following Amazon standards) blocking the site over new rules regarding profanity references... Most frequently cited examples were prior coverage of the Brain F*** Scheduler for the Linux kernel, mostly years old articles of quoting interesting Linus Torvalds commentary on the Linux kernel mailing list with his passionate statements, and other similar statements as well as reader comments in the forums. One well cited example is Linus Torvalds past comments regarding NVIDIA and their at the time proprietary Linux drivers.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Most of the references being expressive words not taken into any context by the ad network algorithms. So even after having to remove content and censor select words to try to address the issue, their appeal process remains ongoing while strangling the site's sustainability in the process.&lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;So if you would like to help and in the process make the site less reliant on -- annoying -- ad networks, please consider joining Phoronix Premium this holiday season to help the site survive.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For the next week, you can enjoy discounted rates for Phoronix Premium: $30 USD for an annual subscription compared to the current rate of $45 per year or $150 USD for a lifetime subscription, down from the rate otherwise of $250.&lt;/strong&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;strong&gt;If you would like to take advantage of this promotion:&lt;/strong&gt; first ensure you need to be registered on the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/forums/"&gt;Phoronix Forums&lt;/a&gt; as they are what handles the subscription/user management aspect. There is not any integrated sales support in there so the normal prices of premium subscriptions are listed there still. To get the discounted deals of $30 for a year subscription or $150 for a lifetime subscription, PayPal the amount of money to &lt;u&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="65080c060d04000925150d0a170a0b0c1d4b060a08"&gt;[email&amp;#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/u&gt; (or you can &lt;a href="https://www.paypal.com/donate/?hosted_button_id=EA79CCDLNFJNW"&gt;use this PayPal link&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://buy.stripe.com/28o02d1yG1Lp8H67ss"&gt;this Stripe link&lt;/a&gt; to avoid any annoying PayPal behavior). Via Stripe is also the best way if just wanting to contribute via credit card and/or avoiding a PayPal account. Sending any amount extra will be treated as a tip or can be renewed for multiple years if desired, thanks! Then as soon as possible I will have activated your forum account to premium status. If your payment email address is different from your forum's registered email address, please include your forum username when making the payment.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Email me at &lt;u&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="90fdf9f3f8f1f5fcd0e0f8ffe2fffef9e8bef3fffd"&gt;[email&amp;#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/u&gt; with any questions. I am always happy to entertain corporate subscriptions / custom offers or similar as well to help support the site and its continued operations.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Thank you for your support considerations and happy holidays.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
